This strategy and backtest are strictly published as a case study on quantitative modeling, structural over-filtering, and sample size bias. It is NOT presented as a functional model for live market execution.

📌 Theoretical Architecture
The algorithm was engineered to identify short-term price exhaustion by targeting specific structural conditions:

Multi-Timeframe Alignment: Requires price deviation across three independent benchmarks simultaneously (Session VWAP, 12-period, and 78-period Rolling VWAP).

Volume Climax: Mandates institutional volume expansion relative to historical moving averages.

Regime Filter: Uses ADX thresholds to restrict operation exclusively to non-trending/ranging market conditions.

🔬 Empirical Backtest Analysis (5-Year High-Frequency Data)
Evaluated across S&P 500 / NASDAQ 100 intraday data (~330,000 candles over 5 years):

Total Executed Trades: 20

Data Horizon: August 2019 – August 2024

Execution Frequency: ~4 setups per calendar year

💡 My View: Quantitative Limitations
In my opinion, this script serves as a clear demonstration of why quantitative models cannot be evaluated solely on trade outcomes without considering sample distribution:

Insignificant Sample Size: Generating only 20 trade entries across 330,000+ price bars provides zero statistical significance. The sample size is far too small to establish market edge or structural validity.

Anomalous Triggering: Due to extremely restrictive confluence conditions, setups almost exclusively fired during macro volatility spikes (such as the March 2020 liquidity shock and August 2024 volatility clusters).

Execution Friction: High-precision filters operating on tiny price moves remain highly vulnerable to real-world market friction, including spread expansion, execution slippage, and broker fees.

🎯 Conclusion
The primary purpose of sharing this repository is to demonstrate how stackable confluence parameters reduce trade frequency to statistically irrelevant levels. Always evaluate total trade sample sizes and execution dynamics when analyzing quantitative scripts.
